<p>Hi, thanks for the feedback!</p>
<p>To answer your question: it's dammerung. As for the length, some of that is formatting, although you might be right in that I could trim some of the fat.</p>
<p>The main revelations throughout the skip:</p>
<ul>
<li>They wouldn't disclose the information because it's extremely traumatic, although this is in actuality a front perpetrated by the crystal to drive home how awful it is</li>
<li>The last interview is Dr. Holden (now deceased) being interviewed by a site director.</li>
<li>The crystal is sentient, and has its own agenda</li>
</ul>
<p>The twist:</p>
<ul>
<li>The reanimated individuals aren't actually &quot;themselves&quot; anymore - but instead the crystal impersonating the deceased to further its own end. It's highjacking their bodies, using its knowledge of DAMMERUNG in an attempt to convince people to commit suicide and become another instance of SCP-XXXX-1. (This isn't incorporated into the draft yet.)</li>
<li>Holden's (rather depressing arc) is that he is eventually convinced by the object. He's confronted with a choice: an eternity of suffering through a conventional death (DAMMERUNG), or a total cessation of consciousness forever by handing over his body to the crystal. In comparison, the latter isn't too bad.</li>
<li>Why should a Foundation Researcher believe an anomalous object with its own agenda? Well, it gets a bit meta here I suppose. Holden is eventually convinced because a corpse (or rather, the crystal) shows him the classified DAMMERUNG SCP article. This way, the crosslink isn't just window dressing - it's to drive the story forward and therefore a reason for its inclusion.</li>
<li>Also, I'm going to cross-test the corpses with 682 and 096 (I'm joking, don't worry)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Edit:</strong> I just rechecked the length. Without the formatting, it's 4008 words.</p>

