<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">►&nbsp;my&nbsp;detailed&nbsp;thoughts◄</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Cognitohazardous crystalline object capable of reanimating (fully sapient) corpses.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Why does this need a cognitohazard? It’s already capable of reanimating the dead… isn’t that anomalous enough?</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Healthy individuals, when exposed to the object through continuous physical contact (or as is discovered later, simply being within its general proximity) become transfixed with SCP-XXXX and develop paranoia and suicidal ideation. If left uninterrupted, they will commit suicide, therefore becoming an instance of SCP-XXXX-1.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Far too many compulsion effects on the wiki, and this one even makes you crazy and then you kill yourself… honestly, and I don’t mean to be harsh, but this is cliched and I would cut this anomalous effect.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>The experiment logs generally follow Dr. Holden in termination tests on instances of SCP-XXXX-1. Whilst they aren't immune to physical damage, they feel no pain and remain functional far past the point of a normal human (i.e. an instance of SCP-XXXX-1 can survive decapitation, and its body can continue to move independently of the head despite severance of the nervous system/spinal cord).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>//This seems bleak as hell. Why are there termination tests at all? I would suggest summarizing instead of making a test log. Like “Through research it has been determined that SCP-XXXX-1, once reanimated, do not register pain and function long past significant damage that would otherwise prove fatal in a normal human being.<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-751177-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-751177-1')">1</a></sup></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Moreover, it eventually starts to become apparent that there's more to the objects nature than researchers initially thought.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Like what?</em></p>
<hr /></div>
</div>
</div>
<p><strong><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Overall</span></strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>I think the idea has merit, but I would limit the anomalous effect to the reanimation of the dead. And then use interviews with reanimated subjects to discover the hidden effects.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>One big question I have: How did the Foundation even discover this anomaly? Or its anomalous effects? Is there any history attached to it? Or did it just appear one day?</li>
</ul>
