<p>Hi! Thanks for your feedback</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">▼&nbsp;Here's&nbsp;a&nbsp;detailed&nbsp;response&nbsp;to&nbsp;your&nbsp;questions</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">▲&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p><em>Why does this need a cognitohazard? It’s already capable of reanimating the dead…<br />
isn't that anomalous enough?</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>I see what you mean. After reading about common pitfalls new authors fall into, I'm removing all anomalous attributes relating to compulsion/cognitohazards</p>
<blockquote>
<p><em>This seems bleak as hell. Why are there termination tests at all? I would suggest<br />
summarizing instead of making a test log. Like “Through research it has been determined that<br />
SCP-XXXX-1, once reanimated, do not register pain and function long past significant damage<br />
would otherwise prove fatal in a normal human being</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>To be honest, bleak was the tone I was going for. As for the termination tests, they're simply to assess how much damage an instance of SCP-XXXX-1 can take before expiring. I did think of &quot;The Foundation is cold, not cruel&quot; when writing this part. However, I thought because reanimated individuals are anomalous entities, in the eyes of the Foundation no longer human. I do see your point, though. I'll do away with lengthy test logs and summarise it like you said.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><em>Like what?</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>The general idea is there's going to be a revelation at the end (which is foreshadowed throughout the article). Essentially, reanimated individuals are <em>not</em> the previously deceased, merely masquerading as them. The crystal is sentient, and anomalously impersonating dead individuals in attempt to convince more people to give their bodies to the crystal.</p>
<p>This is where SCP-2718 comes in. The crystal uses its knowledge of the afterlife to horrify those it speaks to (via reanimated individuals). It offers them a choice: die conventionally, and you'll rot for all eternity in agonising pain <em>or</em> agree to be reanimated (in which case they will simply cease to exist, which is far less bleak in comparison).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>One big question I have: How did the Foundation even discover this anomaly? Or its<br />
anomolous effects? Is there any history attached to it? Or did it just appear one day?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Initially, I thought the origins of the crystal would be related to a cult. However, that's cliche and boring. Instead, the object was a family heirloom that held a lot of sentimental value to an individual. Consequently, they died holding the object in a hospice. Then, they came back - which is where the Foundation comes in. I just wasn't sure how to include this origin when the object was a cognitohazard that drove people to suicide. Luckily, that's not a problem anymore. Thanks to your advice, I've done away with the cognitohazard/compulsion angle.</p>
<p>As for its history and origin of its anomalous abilities, I felt like explaining too much just negates the mystery.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

