					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5336470&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605818720" alt="cybersqyd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5336470)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;" >cybersqyd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1605794048 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">19 Nov 2020 13:54</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4822409">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The Crystal is sentient, and an entity inhabits it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is the first the crystal is mentioned and it's not exactly clear what it's role is here: I'm assuming it's the thing that actually does the reanimation?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and it's eventually discovered he is not who he says he is.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>who is he? the implication i'm getting here is that it doesn't really matter cos the focus after this switches to the crystal but?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It's a demonic entity trapped within the Crystal, reduced to a parasite, feeding off life-energy to survive.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My concern with this big reveal here is that it feels like it'd produce a reaction best described as 'okay but so what?'</p>
<hr />
<p>I think my big concern with this is it's structured like a series of big reveals but none of the reveals feel like they have real narrative weight: the problem is that a lot of the time, the reveal doesn't really significantly change our understanding of what's happening. Like, consider the hivemind reveal: we find out it's a parasitic life form and our reaction then is 'huh that's weird' but there's not really any further implications from there? This is in part because it's entire motivation is 'trying to survive', which like, everything does; as opposed to like, trying to achieve a specific aim.</p>
<p>It also feels like it really needs to tap into some kinda primal fear or emotion here: like, if this was centred around the fear of being replaced or something, it'd end up feeling a lot more impactful to me. As it is, there feels like this has a real lack of emotional response here: all of this happens but it never feels like it's impactful.</p>
</div>
</div>
